WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.306 --> 00:00:04.520
- What's up Believe Nation?

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.614
I started the Mentor
Me series with the goal

00:00:06.614 --> 00:00:09.301
to try to hang your own
people a little bit longer

00:00:09.301 --> 00:00:10.770
who've done more than us.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.705
And by staying with them
and learning from them,

00:00:13.705 --> 00:00:15.214
hopefully some of their strategies,

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:17.908
their wisdom, their mindset, seeps into us

00:00:17.908 --> 00:00:21.089
to help us become the
best version of ourselves.

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:22.754
So today we're going to
learn from Mark Cuban

00:00:22.754 --> 00:00:24.666
on his investing strategies.

00:00:24.666 --> 00:00:25.859
Mentor me Mark.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.721
Also as always guys, as you're watching,

00:00:27.721 --> 00:00:30.008
if Mark says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:30.008 --> 00:00:31.648
please leave it down the comments below.

00:00:31.648 --> 00:00:32.737
Put quotes around it as well

00:00:32.737 --> 00:00:34.372
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:34.372 --> 00:00:35.718
And when you write it down,

00:00:35.718 --> 00:00:38.694
it's more likely to lock
in for yourself as well.

00:00:38.694 --> 00:00:39.729
Enjoy.

00:00:39.729 --> 00:00:43.229
(inspiring opening music)

00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:55.368
- What if I offered you $1.25 million

00:00:55.368 --> 00:00:56.206
to sell the whole company?

00:00:56.206 --> 00:00:59.337
- Mark, you're saying
screw the other sharks.

00:00:59.337 --> 00:01:00.296
- Yup.

00:01:00.296 --> 00:01:01.603
I like what you guys are doing.

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:03.799
I want you to come back
to me with a percentage.

00:01:03.799 --> 00:01:05.236
I don't want you to
even talk to these guys.

00:01:05.236 --> 00:01:08.598
- You're asking him to take
that option with you right now

00:01:08.598 --> 00:01:09.567
and ignore us?

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:10.587
You're not a very nice guy.

00:01:10.587 --> 00:01:12.221
- It's not about me being wonderful.

00:01:12.221 --> 00:01:13.676
- No, no, I have that title, thank you.

00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:15.750
- It's about making a
business decision, right?

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:16.598
- Hi, I'm Mark Cuban.

00:01:16.598 --> 00:01:17.883
I'm a bully, look at me.

00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:19.255
- What's that about, Mark?

00:01:19.255 --> 00:01:21.413
- Like I said guys,
you got to pay to play.

00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:23.203
- There's tension in
this room, I can feel it.

00:01:23.203 --> 00:01:24.269
- It's offensive.

00:01:24.269 --> 00:01:25.207
- You got a shot here, right?

00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:26.739
Don't let these guys drag you down.

00:01:26.739 --> 00:01:27.967
- Are you really interested in this?

00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:28.980
- I like the idea.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.651
- You really think cooking
bacon bedside is a good idea?

00:01:32.651 --> 00:01:34.774
- I love your passion,
I love your knowledge.

00:01:34.774 --> 00:01:35.866
- I'm waiting for the but.

00:01:35.866 --> 00:01:37.085
- Yeah, there's a but coming.

00:01:37.085 --> 00:01:40.220
- You've got a 24-second
shot clock, $750,000.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.131
- I've never seen greed like that.

00:01:41.131 --> 00:01:42.367
That's unbelievable greed.

00:01:42.367 --> 00:01:43.870
- You broke up with your potential partner

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.310
without even looking at him.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:46.143
- You're a savage.

00:01:46.143 --> 00:01:49.484
- I'm just a lean mean
shark machine, baby.

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:51.877
If I say yes to whatever your counter is,

00:01:51.877 --> 00:01:54.273
you have to tell me that
you will say yes immediately

00:01:54.273 --> 00:01:55.211
and we have a deal.

00:01:55.211 --> 00:01:56.246
- $100,000.

00:01:56.246 --> 00:01:57.161
- Now wait a minute.

00:01:57.161 --> 00:01:58.556
- For 20% equity.

00:01:58.556 --> 00:01:59.498
- Done, done and done.

00:01:59.498 --> 00:02:00.432
- Crap.

00:02:00.432 --> 00:02:01.265
- you like that, Barbara?

00:02:01.265 --> 00:02:03.163
- I don't like it one bit.

00:02:03.163 --> 00:02:04.720
That was so damn smart.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.160
- No, but that's a compliment to you.

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.904
- Barbara, I screwed you,
but it's a compliment to you.

00:02:08.904 --> 00:02:10.069
- I feel really good about it.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:11.672
- This isn't the rose garden.

00:02:11.672 --> 00:02:13.704
It's the shark tank and you know what,

00:02:13.704 --> 00:02:15.219
sometimes you got to take the bite

00:02:15.219 --> 00:02:17.756
and sometimes you're the chum.

00:02:17.756 --> 00:02:20.759
- So what is it that you're
really looking for, Mark,

00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:24.578
when you choose to say I'm in,
I'm going to make you an offer?

00:02:24.578 --> 00:02:27.432
- I really don't have any one thing.

00:02:27.432 --> 00:02:30.475
I try to be objective about each company,

00:02:30.475 --> 00:02:33.634
but I guess I look for
something that's differentiated.

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:35.122
I look for something that's timely.

00:02:35.122 --> 00:02:38.518
I look for somebody who I
think can run the company,

00:02:38.518 --> 00:02:39.679
that's committed to it.

00:02:39.679 --> 00:02:41.573
I look for someone who
does their homework.

00:02:41.573 --> 00:02:43.150
And I look to see if
I can add value to it.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.618
Is it something that works for me?

00:02:45.618 --> 00:02:48.110
Because if it's something,
the frozen food business,

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.328
what big chefs are seeing--

00:02:50.328 --> 00:02:53.096
- Not your thing, not
your area of connections?

00:02:53.096 --> 00:02:56.005
- You know what, it's
not so much connections.

00:02:56.005 --> 00:02:59.585
It's more okay, it means I'm
going to have to put in the time

00:02:59.585 --> 00:03:01.214
to work at this

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:03.245
because otherwise it's not
fair to the entrepreneur

00:03:03.245 --> 00:03:04.491
and do I have the time?

00:03:04.491 --> 00:03:07.091
And that time, and you hear
me say it over and over again,

00:03:07.091 --> 00:03:09.141
time is my biggest constraint.

00:03:09.141 --> 00:03:11.497
It's more valuable to me than money.

00:03:11.497 --> 00:03:13.334
It's the one thing you can't get back.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:15.024
And look, I'm not here to tell you

00:03:15.024 --> 00:03:17.752
that time has always been
my most valuable asset.

00:03:17.752 --> 00:03:19.131
When I was sleeping on the floor,

00:03:19.131 --> 00:03:21.464
I had plenty of time, right?

00:03:23.298 --> 00:03:25.553
As you get older, I think
more than anything else,

00:03:25.553 --> 00:03:27.029
and as you have a family,

00:03:27.029 --> 00:03:28.686
time becomes your most valuable asset

00:03:28.686 --> 00:03:31.126
and that as much as anything else

00:03:31.126 --> 00:03:32.742
impacts the investments I make.

00:03:32.742 --> 00:03:35.487
- The markets have been going
through these wild gyrations.

00:03:35.487 --> 00:03:36.673
- Right.

00:03:36.673 --> 00:03:40.696
- Don't seem to have a clear
sense of what direction is up.

00:03:40.696 --> 00:03:42.209
How do you interpret that as somebody

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:43.320
who's spent a lot of time
playing in the markets?

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.577
- Well, put on my manage
a portfolio trader hat,

00:03:46.577 --> 00:03:50.322
and not so much a trader but
manage a portfolio, it's great.

00:03:50.322 --> 00:03:54.033
Anytime there's volatility
and anytime there's change,

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:55.274
there's opportunity.

00:03:55.274 --> 00:03:56.219
Now I say it's great

00:03:56.219 --> 00:03:58.189
because I've taken a whole
different approach to investing

00:03:58.189 --> 00:03:59.677
than most people.

00:03:59.677 --> 00:04:01.462
I think buy and hold
is a crock of (bleep).

00:04:01.462 --> 00:04:04.974
I think the idea that you
always have to invest your cash

00:04:04.974 --> 00:04:06.318
is not far behind.

00:04:06.318 --> 00:04:10.124
And so I've always been
of the attitude that

00:04:10.124 --> 00:04:14.087
unless you really have a
commitment to something,

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.631
just keep your money and cash

00:04:15.631 --> 00:04:17.997
knowing that at some point in time,

00:04:17.997 --> 00:04:20.485
there's going to be a week
or two like we've had.

00:04:20.485 --> 00:04:21.338
- But how much time,

00:04:21.338 --> 00:04:22.761
I mean you've got a lot
going on in your life,

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:25.995
how much time do you have to
actively manage your portfolio

00:04:25.995 --> 00:04:27.939
for these 500 point market swings?

00:04:27.939 --> 00:04:28.772
- Well see that's the whole thing.

00:04:28.772 --> 00:04:29.958
I don't have to spend much time

00:04:29.958 --> 00:04:32.375
until it hits the fan, right?

00:04:33.455 --> 00:04:34.887
- A lot are hitting the fan this week.

00:04:34.887 --> 00:04:36.992
- That's exactly right and
that's where the opportunity is.

00:04:36.992 --> 00:04:38.504
I won't look at my portfolio

00:04:38.504 --> 00:04:43.342
or I get a one-line,
one-number statement everyday

00:04:43.342 --> 00:04:47.533
from my bank and that tells
me if anything weird happened

00:04:47.533 --> 00:04:48.551
and I won't even look at it,

00:04:48.551 --> 00:04:50.734
but then when everything
starts getting crazy,

00:04:50.734 --> 00:04:52.862
I call it the world series of investing,

00:04:52.862 --> 00:04:54.706
that's when you start digging in

00:04:54.706 --> 00:04:57.597
and it's because of the
approach that I take.

00:04:57.597 --> 00:05:02.363
So back in 2006 and 2007,
I was writing blogs saying,

00:05:02.363 --> 00:05:05.627
"Look, the stock market is for suckers."

00:05:05.627 --> 00:05:06.998
When you sit down at the business table,

00:05:06.998 --> 00:05:08.362
you always look for the sucker.

00:05:08.362 --> 00:05:10.029
And if you don't see it, it's you.

00:05:10.029 --> 00:05:11.983
And you've got all these
professional people

00:05:11.983 --> 00:05:14.414
on the other side of the trades.

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:16.333
When I started trading
stocks in the early '90s

00:05:16.333 --> 00:05:18.548
after I sold my first company,

00:05:18.548 --> 00:05:21.645
you could understand different
elements of the market better

00:05:21.645 --> 00:05:22.921
than the professionals.

00:05:22.921 --> 00:05:25.871
So I can understand new technology

00:05:25.871 --> 00:05:27.501
from Wellfleet and SynOptics

00:05:27.501 --> 00:05:29.397
and all these old technology companies

00:05:29.397 --> 00:05:30.685
better than the traders.

00:05:30.685 --> 00:05:33.797
Today, there's so much money
in these huge hedge funds

00:05:33.797 --> 00:05:36.511
and they have such professional research

00:05:36.511 --> 00:05:37.810
and in-depth research,

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.131
there really aren't any advantages

00:05:40.131 --> 00:05:41.734
for the individual traders

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.230
and so my approach has always been

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:48.055
unless I know something
specific, put it in cash.

00:05:48.055 --> 00:05:49.210
- And so what you investing in?

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:52.521
What are the areas that you feel you know?

00:05:52.521 --> 00:05:54.227
- In 2008 and 2009,

00:05:54.227 --> 00:05:57.839
I put everything into MLPs and Emirates,

00:05:57.839 --> 00:05:59.045
mortgage back security,

00:05:59.045 --> 00:06:01.693
the ones that I thought
were the better companies

00:06:01.693 --> 00:06:03.359
and I just piled in.

00:06:03.359 --> 00:06:06.092
And I also piled into Australian bonds

00:06:06.092 --> 00:06:08.520
because I thought the economy
was good next to China.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:09.807
It was my way of playing China.

00:06:09.807 --> 00:06:11.119
- So you make one-way bets.

00:06:11.119 --> 00:06:13.335
This isn't portfolio balancing
you're talking about.

00:06:13.335 --> 00:06:16.079
- No, all that asset
management, diversification,

00:06:16.079 --> 00:06:17.146
that's for idiots, right,

00:06:17.146 --> 00:06:19.733
because you can't diversify enough

00:06:19.733 --> 00:06:20.901
to know what you're doing.

00:06:20.901 --> 00:06:22.927
What I did was I said,

00:06:22.927 --> 00:06:25.504
"Self, there's going to
be a lot of volatility."

00:06:25.504 --> 00:06:28.064
So I took a little bit of money

00:06:28.064 --> 00:06:32.231
and bought out of the money
calls on the spider calls.

00:06:33.255 --> 00:06:36.673
I bought Standards &amp; Poor's, the S&amp;P 500,

00:06:36.673 --> 00:06:38.113
and I did the same thing on the diamonds

00:06:38.113 --> 00:06:41.239
which is the Dow Jones
so I bought those along

00:06:41.239 --> 00:06:43.512
when the stock price was cratered

00:06:43.512 --> 00:06:45.067
and then when we had the big, not today,

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:47.866
but the big run up before,
I bought a bunch of puts

00:06:47.866 --> 00:06:49.298
knowing that even though I was paying

00:06:49.298 --> 00:06:51.046
for a lot of volatility

00:06:51.046 --> 00:06:54.106
that I thought there would be
a lot of swings in the market.

00:06:54.106 --> 00:06:55.523
- So you're just spending on volatility.

00:06:55.523 --> 00:06:56.356
- I'm just betting on volatility.

00:06:56.356 --> 00:06:58.743
- And how long is that
going to last in your view?

00:06:58.743 --> 00:07:00.974
- No idea and that's
the whole thing, right?

00:07:00.974 --> 00:07:04.119
People aren't buying intrinsic
value in companies anymore.

00:07:04.119 --> 00:07:06.718
The whole Warren Buffett
approach works great for Warren

00:07:06.718 --> 00:07:10.382
because he can put $3
billion into an investment

00:07:10.382 --> 00:07:11.964
and take a whole different approach

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:14.791
than a John or Sally Doe
investor who can't do that.

00:07:14.791 --> 00:07:17.645
- So you say buy and hold
is a crock of something,

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:19.728
I can't remember exactly,

00:07:20.592 --> 00:07:23.677
and you said the portfolio
balancing is a waste of time

00:07:23.677 --> 00:07:26.767
so what are John and
Sally Doe supposed to do?

00:07:26.767 --> 00:07:28.605
- It depends on how much
money you have, right?

00:07:28.605 --> 00:07:30.452
So the best way to get a return,

00:07:30.452 --> 00:07:32.958
let's just say you have $50,000 in cash.

00:07:32.958 --> 00:07:36.717
It could be 10, it could
be 100, but let's use 50.

00:07:36.717 --> 00:07:38.656
The first thing I would do is
pay off all your credit cards

00:07:38.656 --> 00:07:40.393
'cause that's costing you 18%.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:41.935
That's what John and
Sally should do first,

00:07:41.935 --> 00:07:43.254
most don't, right?

00:07:43.254 --> 00:07:46.094
There's more credit card
than student loan debt

00:07:46.094 --> 00:07:48.340
relative to personal income
than there's even been

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:50.664
in the history of the United States.

00:07:50.664 --> 00:07:51.647
The second thing you do

00:07:51.647 --> 00:07:54.148
is you use the
transactional value of cash.

00:07:54.148 --> 00:07:56.980
A lot of people say, "You're
losing money to inflation

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:58.710
"when you just have cash in the bank."

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.010
I completely disagree.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:02.915
I know I'm buying a bunch
of toothpaste for my family.

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:03.882
I know toilet paper.

00:08:03.882 --> 00:08:06.175
I know all the consumables that I have.

00:08:06.175 --> 00:08:08.902
I can take my cash and
go get a better value.

00:08:08.902 --> 00:08:10.335
I can take my cash and go buy--

00:08:10.335 --> 00:08:11.348
- You store up?

00:08:11.348 --> 00:08:12.783
- Yeah, yeah, why not?

00:08:12.783 --> 00:08:16.216
How much space does toothpaste take?

00:08:16.216 --> 00:08:17.122
But that's the whole thing.

00:08:17.122 --> 00:08:19.441
The time it takes just to
figure out your budget,

00:08:19.441 --> 00:08:20.457
which nobody does,

00:08:20.457 --> 00:08:23.158
the time it takes just to
analyze your spending habits,

00:08:23.158 --> 00:08:24.472
you can get a better return

00:08:24.472 --> 00:08:26.100
and you'll end up with more cash

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:29.506
than trying to fight the
John Paulsons of the world

00:08:29.506 --> 00:08:32.345
and these guys who have
hundreds of analysts

00:08:32.345 --> 00:08:33.272
who are working for them.

00:08:33.272 --> 00:08:34.993
Why would you try to do better than them?

00:08:34.993 --> 00:08:35.826
You can't.
- You can't win.

00:08:35.826 --> 00:08:38.917
You can't and now it's 100 times worse

00:08:38.917 --> 00:08:41.472
because of all the program trading.

00:08:41.472 --> 00:08:42.727
- But if you have enough money

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:45.072
that you have to put some of it somewhere,

00:08:45.072 --> 00:08:46.586
to park some of it somewhere

00:08:46.586 --> 00:08:48.084
and hope to make a return on it,

00:08:48.084 --> 00:08:50.382
where do you put it?

00:08:50.382 --> 00:08:51.504
- What I do?

00:08:51.504 --> 00:08:53.563
I don't think you have
to make a return on it

00:08:53.563 --> 00:08:55.484
because I'd rather sleep well at night.

00:08:55.484 --> 00:08:57.052
I put it in cash literally.

00:08:57.052 --> 00:08:58.599
Now obviously I'm in a different position

00:08:58.599 --> 00:09:01.570
where I don't have to live
off of the interest paying me

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:03.670
one basis point off of cash, right?

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:08.449
But at the same time, having
that dry powder, if you will,

00:09:08.449 --> 00:09:12.170
available when weeks like today
or weeks like this week hit

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:13.866
and days like yesterday and today happen--

00:09:13.866 --> 00:09:14.708
- And you see an opportunity.

00:09:14.708 --> 00:09:17.219
- And you see an opportunity
then you're ready to take it

00:09:17.219 --> 00:09:18.897
as opposed to saying, "I can't get out.

00:09:18.897 --> 00:09:19.852
"What am I going to do?"

00:09:19.852 --> 00:09:22.083
And I'll tell you the
other part that's better,

00:09:22.083 --> 00:09:23.874
you're going to cut your health cost

00:09:23.874 --> 00:09:25.950
because you're going to sleep
a whole lot better at night.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:26.783
- Do you sleep well?

00:09:26.783 --> 00:09:28.186
- I sleep like a baby.

00:09:28.186 --> 00:09:30.011
Don't use credit cards.

00:09:30.011 --> 00:09:32.566
Of all the investments you'll ever make,

00:09:32.566 --> 00:09:36.733
paying 19% or 18% on something
is easily the dumbest.

00:09:41.309 --> 00:09:43.731
That's money that you should
be keeping, you can be saving.

00:09:43.731 --> 00:09:44.720
It's a different world now

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:47.240
because you can be a smart consumer.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:49.775
You have the ability to price
check anything on Amazon

00:09:49.775 --> 00:09:53.341
or Walmart.com and you know
what you need to save for

00:09:53.341 --> 00:09:54.635
or put it on law away with them now.

00:09:54.635 --> 00:09:58.589
It ended up I had so
many credit cards cut up.

00:09:58.589 --> 00:10:01.038
I was cleaning up some stuff
and saw my old credit report.

00:10:01.038 --> 00:10:02.455
Sears, AMX, Visa.

00:10:08.114 --> 00:10:09.635
The situation I was in was like

00:10:09.635 --> 00:10:12.184
anybody who would give me
a credit card I would use

00:10:12.184 --> 00:10:16.113
because I didn't have any
money so I needed to use it.

00:10:16.113 --> 00:10:18.949
Literally, someone's
flipping the old chunks

00:10:18.949 --> 00:10:21.181
of my old credit cards somewhere.

00:10:21.181 --> 00:10:22.435
Stay away from credit.

00:10:22.435 --> 00:10:23.722
If you have a 401(k) match

00:10:23.722 --> 00:10:24.555
and you don't do it

00:10:24.555 --> 00:10:26.445
for as much as you can
possibly force yourself to do,

00:10:26.445 --> 00:10:27.778
you're an idiot.

00:10:31.978 --> 00:10:33.603
If you don't have a 401(k)

00:10:33.603 --> 00:10:38.145
then there's so many esoteric
dumb ass banking rules, right,

00:10:38.145 --> 00:10:41.248
where you can't just put
$10 or $20 into the bank

00:10:41.248 --> 00:10:42.776
because of all the service fees,

00:10:42.776 --> 00:10:44.576
they're going to crush you, right?

00:10:44.576 --> 00:10:46.814
And so there's a variety of new things

00:10:46.814 --> 00:10:48.382
and opportunities that are coming up

00:10:48.382 --> 00:10:52.658
where if you can get yourself
to put in $10 a week,

00:10:52.658 --> 00:10:54.595
$20 a week, $100 a month,

00:10:54.595 --> 00:10:57.419
if you're 21 and you put in $100 a month

00:10:57.419 --> 00:11:00.873
and you put it into a
Vanguard Index Fund let's say,

00:11:00.873 --> 00:11:02.712
hopefully you'll be able
to increase those amounts

00:11:02.712 --> 00:11:04.965
and so literally if you can train yourself

00:11:04.965 --> 00:11:08.011
to save 100 bucks a month
and grow it as you earn more,

00:11:08.011 --> 00:11:10.447
you're going to be a millionaire
by the time you retire.

00:11:10.447 --> 00:11:12.739
If you don't, you're going
to be in the same boat

00:11:12.739 --> 00:11:16.207
I was at 27 and broke or
trying to figure it out

00:11:16.207 --> 00:11:19.485
or others at 50 or 60 will have nothing.

00:11:19.485 --> 00:11:24.118
The opportunities exist
and if you can invest in

00:11:24.118 --> 00:11:27.586
just a stock index fund, an SPX fund,

00:11:27.586 --> 00:11:31.146
Vanguard I mentioned because
it's the lowest cost,

00:11:31.146 --> 00:11:34.563
do it, do it, do it, do it, do it, do it.

00:11:35.632 --> 00:11:36.586
Two less beers.

00:11:36.586 --> 00:11:38.069
No, these days one less beer.

00:11:38.069 --> 00:11:40.983
One less fireball shot
and you can be rich.

00:11:40.983 --> 00:11:44.422
Have those first five
fireballs, but those last two,

00:11:44.422 --> 00:11:45.653
that's for the future.

00:11:45.653 --> 00:11:47.047
We sell the company

00:11:47.047 --> 00:11:49.648
and I don't remember the
exact price of Yahoo stocks.

00:11:49.648 --> 00:11:51.336
It was like $250.

00:11:51.336 --> 00:11:53.336
And from my perspective,

00:11:54.253 --> 00:11:57.731
this was April of 2000 when it closed,

00:11:57.731 --> 00:12:02.067
and I don't see the stock
market just going up forever.

00:12:02.067 --> 00:12:04.056
Literally, the Nasdaq just passed 5,000

00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:07.408
which is where it was back then in 2000.

00:12:07.408 --> 00:12:09.065
Like I said, I traded stocks.

00:12:09.065 --> 00:12:11.604
I started and sold a hedge fund

00:12:11.604 --> 00:12:14.216
and so I understood what
happens in the market

00:12:14.216 --> 00:12:17.290
and gravy trains don't last
forever and so I'm like,

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:19.344
"Look, this was crazy."

00:12:19.344 --> 00:12:20.565
It's always been a little crazy,

00:12:20.565 --> 00:12:22.360
but it's getting even too heated.

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:24.759
And so I had to wait six months

00:12:24.759 --> 00:12:26.708
before I could hedge my stock,

00:12:26.708 --> 00:12:28.775
meaning buy an offset
in case it went down,

00:12:28.775 --> 00:12:31.757
so I literally took every penny I had,

00:12:31.757 --> 00:12:34.361
20 some million dollars in cash,

00:12:34.361 --> 00:12:38.528
and I put it on an index
against the entire market

00:12:40.998 --> 00:12:43.538
and that put was for six months,

00:12:43.538 --> 00:12:45.243
knowing that it got me to the point

00:12:45.243 --> 00:12:48.986
where I can hedge all my Yahoo stock

00:12:48.986 --> 00:12:51.971
but if the market crashed prior to that,

00:12:51.971 --> 00:12:54.819
I was as good as I could be.

00:12:54.819 --> 00:12:57.338
The market didn't crash.

00:12:57.338 --> 00:12:59.114
I lost every penny,

00:12:59.114 --> 00:13:01.781
but the minute I was allowed to,

00:13:02.994 --> 00:13:07.161
I hedged everything,
everything, everything, right?

00:13:08.385 --> 00:13:10.346
Everybody thought I was an idiot.

00:13:10.346 --> 00:13:13.725
There's literally a CNBC
interview where he said,

00:13:13.725 --> 00:13:15.582
"Mark, don't you feel stupid?

00:13:15.582 --> 00:13:18.484
Yahoo stock is up another $100."

00:13:18.484 --> 00:13:20.445
By this time, I had already sold the Mavs

00:13:20.445 --> 00:13:22.240
and I remember sitting on the team bus

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:23.868
talking to our then coach

00:13:23.868 --> 00:13:27.951
and one of our coaches,
Dell Harris, and he goes,

00:13:28.883 --> 00:13:31.636
"Look at all that money
you left on the table."

00:13:31.636 --> 00:13:33.502
I'm like literally in one day

00:13:33.502 --> 00:13:36.171
the stocks were jumping so crazily,

00:13:36.171 --> 00:13:39.011
one day I paid for the entire Mavericks

00:13:39.011 --> 00:13:42.837
in the stock move of one
day and they're like,

00:13:42.837 --> 00:13:46.309
"But you left all this on the table."

00:13:46.309 --> 00:13:48.381
And I'm like, "Patience, patience."

00:13:48.381 --> 00:13:50.623
I did an interview on CNBC and he goes,

00:13:50.623 --> 00:13:53.694
"Don't you feel horrible that you left

00:13:53.694 --> 00:13:56.039
"a billion plus dollars on the table?"

00:13:56.039 --> 00:13:57.242
It's on tape and I said,

00:13:57.242 --> 00:14:01.820
"Yeah, I feel really bad
flying around in my G5s.

00:14:01.820 --> 00:14:03.570
"It's just horrible."

00:14:05.185 --> 00:14:06.967
But everybody thought I was an idiot,

00:14:06.967 --> 00:14:07.808
even people who worked for me.

00:14:07.808 --> 00:14:09.589
I told everybody and Tod listened,

00:14:09.589 --> 00:14:10.879
some people did and some people didn't

00:14:10.879 --> 00:14:13.060
because every single person
who had worked for us

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:14.447
for more than a couple months

00:14:14.447 --> 00:14:16.366
was at least on paper a millionaire

00:14:16.366 --> 00:14:18.146
and that was 300 plus people

00:14:18.146 --> 00:14:20.980
and so we had a lot of smiley
faces around the office

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:22.264
and I'm like, "Look."

00:14:22.264 --> 00:14:23.547
And they're like, "See you're wrong,

00:14:23.547 --> 00:14:24.751
"the stocks are going up."

00:14:24.751 --> 00:14:28.630
And then the bubble burst and it went boom

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:30.163
and it's unfortunate.

00:14:30.163 --> 00:14:32.054
A lot of people got crushed,

00:14:32.054 --> 00:14:35.529
but because I hedged everything,

00:14:35.529 --> 00:14:38.187
I actually benefited in some respects

00:14:38.187 --> 00:14:40.440
and came out further ahead.

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:41.708
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:41.708 --> 00:14:42.675
I hope you enjoyed.

00:14:42.675 --> 00:14:44.619
I'd love to know what did
you think of the video?

00:14:44.619 --> 00:14:45.745
What was your favorite clip?

00:14:45.745 --> 00:14:48.701
What was the lesson that
struck home most for you?

00:14:48.701 --> 00:14:51.046
What are you going to
immediately apply to your life

00:14:51.046 --> 00:14:52.038
or your business?

00:14:52.038 --> 00:14:53.658
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:14:53.658 --> 00:14:55.741
I'm super curious to figure
out what you guys thought

00:14:55.741 --> 00:14:57.361
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:14:57.361 --> 00:14:58.895
I also want to give a
quick shout out to Frode.

00:14:58.895 --> 00:15:01.384
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:15:01.384 --> 00:15:04.030
Your One Word, and doing
that awesome review

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:05.306
